#{[17592186364142 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364142 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364142 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b8f9a74b-3389-50db-8119-c58d4ce6c953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364143 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364143 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364143 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d10d66c-a477-5bdb-849a-3b4ce2476fbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364144 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364144 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364144 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6d1185e8-43e6-5ead-8df7-18ef3c42fc3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388646 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186388647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388646 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388646 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388646 #edb/ref 1071 "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388646 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4027f898-11dc-5b09-bb9b-58bf2f6ee48d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388646 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9 & 17\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 9 & 17\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -6 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2(1-2{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2+{x}_{3} \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, $2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2+1=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388647 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388647 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388647 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388647 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388647 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186388652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388647 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388647 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388647 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186388652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388647 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "910d4aaa-e4db-5868-9808-87887dacb3b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388648 #edb/ref 1080 "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186388648 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #ed